<section class="method-section content-section" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
  <h2 id="method-heading">Understanding the GMT vs UTC Relationship</h2>
  <p class="method-intro">
    Although GMT and UTC produce identical clock readings for everyday scheduling, understanding their structural background prevents confusion when writing server scripts, reading aviation charts, or configuring network hardware.
  </p>

  <div class="method-grid">
    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 1</p>
      <h3>The Direct 1:1 Equality</h3>
      <p>
        To convert any GMT timestamp to UTC, no calculation is required. <strong>GMT = UTC</strong>. If your schedule specifies 14:00 GMT, your target UTC time is also 14:00 UTC.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 2</p>
      <h3>Server Code & ISO-8601 Format</h3>
      <p>
        In software programming (JavaScript, Python, Go, Java), UTC is represented by the letter <code>Z</code> (standing for Zulu, or zero offset). A timestamp of <code>2026-07-30T12:00:00Z</code> represents 12:00 PM UTC, which is equal to 12:00 PM GMT.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="method-card">
      <p class="eyebrow">Method 3</p>
      <h3>Navigational & Aviation Contexts</h3>
      <p>
        In marine charts and flight logs, GMT was historically used for solar longitude navigation. Modern aviation has fully transitioned to UTC (Zulu time), though air traffic controllers still frequently refer to UTC as GMT in verbal communications.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

