<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     WHEN AND WHERE USED
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="where-used-heading">
  <h2 id="where-used-heading">Where GMT & UTC Are Used Worldwide</h2>
  
  <article style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">1. Web Servers, Cloud Datacenters & API Endpoints</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Modern software architectures almost universally operate on UTC. Cloud service providers such as AWS, Google Cloud, and Microsoft Azure set their internal server clocks to UTC. Using UTC for database records and timestamp logging ensures that transaction order remains perfectly consistent, regardless of where clients or physical server hardware are located.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">2. Aviation, Marine Navigation & Military ("Zulu Time")</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      In global aviation and maritime operations, standardization is critical for safety. Commercial flight plans, meteorological reports, and naval communications run on UTC, referred to in military and aviation terminology as "Zulu Time" (designated by the letter Z). Pilots flying from London to Tokyo or New York to Dubai log all actions in UTC.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">3. Greenwich Solar Baseline & Legal Timekeeping</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      While UTC is the scientific standard, GMT remains the official legal standard in several countries across Western Europe and West Africa (such as Iceland, Senegal, Ghana, and the UK in winter). In legal contracts, broadcasting schedules, and daily conversation in these regions, "GMT" is used as the civil name for offset UTC+0.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">4. Telecommunications & Fiber-Optic Sync</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Global telecommunication switches and undersea fiber optic repeaters synchronize bit rates against atomic UTC standards. Precise nanosecond synchronization prevents data corruption during high-capacity packet switching across oceans.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">5. Global Navigation Satellite Systems (GNSS)</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Global positioning systems including GPS (US), Galileo (Europe), GLONASS (Russia), and BeiDou (China) rely on internal atomic time standards synchronized back to UTC. Position triangulation requires sub-nanosecond clock alignment between orbiting satellites and ground receivers.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">6. Financial Clearinghouses & Interbank Messaging (SWIFT)</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      International wire transfers and monetary settlements processed via SWIFT and Fedwire rely on UTC timestamping to guarantee legal proof of transaction execution times across international banking days.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON MISTAKES
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Mistakes When Converting GMT to UTC</h2>
  
  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 1: Confusing GMT with British Summer Time (BST)</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      The most frequent error occurs during summer months in the UK. Many people assume London is always on "GMT". However, from late March to late October, the UK switches to BST (UTC+1). If you treat UK summer time as GMT, your UTC calculations will be off by exactly one hour.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 2: Subtracting or Adding Hours for GMT to UTC</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Because GMT and UTC represent identical civil hours (UTC+0), some users attempt to apply an offset calculation when none is needed. Remember: 05:00 GMT is 05:00 UTC. There is zero time difference.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 3: Misinterpreting Leap Seconds in Application Code</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      While UTC incorporates leap seconds to stay aligned with solar GMT, most modern computer systems handle leap seconds by "smearing" them across several hours rather than inserting a 61st second (23:59:60). Software developers should rely on system NTP clocks rather than hardcoding leap second logic.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 4: Ignoring Timezone Offsets in Query Strings</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      When submitting date parameters to web APIs, omitting the timezone designator (such as <code>Z</code> or <code>+00:00</code>) causes server parsers to assume local server time, leading to silent databasing errors.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

